d like a beer please and  where is the bathroom in whatever  language and at the other end the other  extreme is the perfectionist who is  frustrated because they can't say  everything they want to say in the  language they're learning and to me the  successful language learners are those  that are in between to me it's perfectly  useless to learn to say 10 phrases in a  language I have never found that I could  go to a country and I've tried I mean  Indonesia or if I was in I don't know  latvia I will inevitably by a phrasebook  look through it try to say those things  but I have no sense of what I'm saying  and the person he doesn't understand me  or comes back in Latvian Indonesian and  I'm lost so to me that is just a false  dream that you can somehow pickup 15  handy phrases and actually do something  with it I don't believe it some people  might that's fine i don't and i think i  mentioned on my blog that I think  language learning is a bit like  gardening only in the sense that it's a  very enjoyable habit it's very good for  one and there is something positive and  constructive that comes out like a  garden or like the ability to  communicate so if you're going to have a  garden you're not just going to plant  three flowers and leave that's not a  garden you have to continue to cultivate  the garden plant more plants or flowers  remove weeds I mean there's an ongoing  activity there I incidentally enjoy  gardening while listening to language  audiobooks on my ipod but that's another  connection between gardening and  language now at the other end of the  other extreme is people who are  perfectionists you know I'm always  frustrated because i can't pronounce  this or because i don't feel comfortable  communicating with people and and so  forth and to me that's just silly you  will never  as comfortable in the second language  whatever your level is on that gradual  progression from zero to comfortable  fluency wherever you are be happy be  happy as long as y